 ### [3D Gaussian Splatting Differentiable Rasterizer](2813-3d-gaussian-splatting-differentiable-rasterizer.md)
-The core rendering engine that projects 3D Gaussian primitives to 2D screen space and accumulates color per pixel using alpha compositing, enabling gradients to flow back to Gaussian parameters (position, scale, rotation, opacity, color). It differs from naive point-sampling approaches by explicitly handling the 2D covariance transformation and alpha blending order, and from production CUDA implementations by providing a Python/PyTorch-based reference implementation that prioritizes gradient correctness and pedagogical clarity over raw throughput. It builds on [3D Gaussian Splatting Rasterization](2812-3d-gaussian-splatting-rasterization.md) by implementing the specific differentiable accumulation logic required for gradient-based optimization.
-
-### [Ultrafast Spatial Texture-Atlas Splatting](3245-ultrafast-spatial-texture-atlas-splatting.md)
-Ultrafast Spatial Texture-Atlas Splatting is a learned sparse scene representation that eliminates runtime neural network inference by baking view-independent appearance into a hardware-accelerated 2D texture atlas. It replaces the volumetric ellipsoids of 3D Gaussian Splatting with oriented 2D Beta kernels and decomposes appearance into low-frequency per-surfel view-dependent color and high-frequency baked residuals. This approach achieves 7–9× faster rendering than standard 3DGS by reducing overdraw through flat kernel regularization and replacing expensive ray-surfel intersection shaders with an exact rational reformulation.
+The 3D Gaussian Splatting Differentiable Rasterizer is the core rendering engine that projects 3D Gaussian primitives to 2D screen space and accumulates color per pixel using alpha compositing, enabling gradients to flow back to Gaussian parameters (position, scale, rotation, opacity, color). It differs from naive point-sampling approaches by explicitly handling the 2D covariance transformation and alpha blending order, and from production CUDA implementations by providing a Python/PyTorch-based reference implementation that prioritizes gradient correctness and pedagogical clarity over raw throughput. It builds on [3D Gaussian Splatting Rasterization](2812-3d-gaussian-splatting-rasterization.md) by implementing the specific differentiable accumulation logic required for gradient-based optimization.
 
